Date: Wed, 13 Oct 93 00:37:10 -0700
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: RECOVERY: What's Glove Got to Do With It?

I just got back from an experience that still has me a bit dazed:
my first game of Novice Coed Slow-pitch Softball.  I haven't ever
played much softball, hadn't played at all in many years, and never
quite got the rules straight.  I didn't bring a glove (oboy -- I get
to borrow a glove and throw right-handed, and I'm a southpaw) or a bat
or cleats (Cleats?  I  thought this was _novice_ softball!), and
everybody else seemed to know the game -- I was sure I was going to be
creamed.  (The rule:  expect the worst; then the reality won't be
worse than that.)

(So why did I sign up?  Because they needed me, because Terry was
enthusiastic and encouraging, and because an old boyfriend of mine
used to play  -- I've done things for worse reasons.)

At this point my thought processes, newly loosened by Branden,
Illuminatus!, and numerous conversations, got jostled clean out of
some old grooves.

I went out there expecting to be judged; to have to put up my usual
front of not being upset about it; to wrestle with my tendency to
judge myself; to be embarrassed about my horrible performance; in
short, to have a pretty miserable time. 

Au contraire.  I was embarrassed all right, but only by the profusion
of praise.  I was astonished at how everyone went out of their way to
find something positive about everything I (or anyone else) did, even
if it didn't work.  If you don't swing at a ball, it's "Good eye!".
If you don't swing at a strike, it's "Now you know how it looks!".  If
you swing and miss, it's "Good swing!".  If you hit a fly ball, it's
"Good hit!".  If you miss a catch, it's "Way to hustle!".  And on and on.

When I went out to play shortstop and stopped between first and
second, nobody seemed to mind; they just smiled and pointed me at the
other cluster of cleat marks.  When I got thrown out at first, the
first base coach praised me for my hustle and apologized for not
telling me to run past the bag.  When I was playing second and ran to
the base because I didn't know what else to do and stood there
confused while runners whipped by (Isn't there a ball out here
somewhere?), I got praise for covering the bag.  I was praised so much
I was dizzy.  I was praised so much that after a while I forgot to be
hard on myself and just cheered on the other players.

I don't remember team sports ever being like this.  I remember
dropping the ball and having people be upset.  I remember striking out
and hearing a half-hearted "Good try".  I remember people yelling at
me where to go and being frustrated when I didn't understand.  I
remember doing a lot of apologizing for not doing the right thing.

We were clobbered, 15 to 5 after 5 innings (the novice rules say the
game stops when one team has a 10-run lead), but it didn't bother me.
What was surprising was that it didn't much seem to bother anybody
else, either.

How much of the experience was that I was experiencing the game
differently -- more willing to accept praise and forgo
self-denigration -- and how much was that the people playing were more
mature and positive?  I may never know.  I do know that for a long
time I have hated competition or the thought of competition, and when
I did play a game I didn't want to win because it meant that the other
person lost (awfully presumptuous of me to assume I knew what losing
was like for them, but that was my Objectivist absolutism at work).
The Extropian poker game was a landmark for me in that regard as well
-- I won and was happy about it.  Tonight I got to lose and be happy
about that too.  At this point I think I'll just enjoy my major
accomplishment -- that I hit the ball all three times I was at bat,
and even got to first base once -- and sleep on it.  And archive this
message, because I'm sure the old groove is waiting for me to fall
back in.

Not much fodder for discussion here, I guess, and not much associated
with Extropianism per se, but I needed to tell somebody about it, and
I couldn't think of anyone else I could tell who would have even a
clue what it means to me.

Wow.
Kennita

