>However, concealed carry of a firearm remains a misdemeanor does it not 
>while unlicensed carry of Mace is a *felony*.

>Duncan Frissell

>Who watched Henry V win at Agincourt last weekend with freemen and 
>crossbows against armored knights.  Kill ratio better than 10 to 1 (French 
>to English).  You almost have to feel sorry for the Feds on the cusp of 
>technological change...

Point of order!

Henry V's army relied on the English _longbow_. It was the French army
that relied on crossbows and armoured knights. 

Couple of interesting points about the longbow:

*       A recent article in New Scientist reported some recent
        research on the efficiency of this rather lethal gadget.
        It takes a lot of practice to learn to hit a target, but
        when used by a specialist a longbow (of the pull available
        in those days) has a range of up to 200 yards and should
        easily punch through the kind of iron plate-over-chain
        armour available to the French nobility.

*       Peak consumption of arrows by Henry V's army was on the
        order of 0.25 million/hour. At Agincourt, there were
        about 20,000 archers firing up to six arrows per minute
        at the enemy.

*       The English army had a clear field to shoot down. For
        the French, it must have been like riding right into
        a battery of miniguns ...

*       The longbow, AFAIK, predated the mounted knight. It was
        just the application that differed.

And about the crossbow:

*       Similar kind of striking power.

*       Takes a lot longer to load than a longbow.

*       Much easier to aim. (No need for the kind of methods
        Henry used to ensure a viable supply of soldiers -- namely
        compulsory longbow practice for all freemen in England
        every Sunday.)

*       Banned on pain of death by various governments throughout
        Europe when they first appeared -- the crossbow was the
        only real "equalizer" a peasant could use to take on a
        mounted knight.

*       Main reason the French lost Agincourt; they failed to perceive
        that on open ground their defensive armour was inadequate. And
        they failed to deploy anything like enough crossbowmen where
        they were needed. If instead they had simply circled round 
        Henry's army -- which was over-extended -- and settled down
        for a campaign of attrition, they'd have been in London by
        the next year.

And finally, the obligatory British urban legend:

*       The real reason for the British nuclear deterrent is not the
        Russians -- it's the French. Yep, _they_'ve got The Bomb
        too, and we've been at war with them for 700 of the past
        thousand years!



