Date: Wed, 13 Oct 1993 10:28:50 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Question: Seasonal Affective Disorder

It's that time of year again when the days get short and the nights
are long, and my personal ailment comes back to haunt me.

I'm pretty certain that I suffer from a syndrome called SAD; seasonal
affective disorder. SAD is recognized as a problem in the UK, and in
parts north (particularly Scandinavia); if you consider England's at 
about the same longitude as various bits of Canada, this is fairly
reasonable.

SAD is a mild, slightly debilitating problem.  Basically, sufferers
get very sleepy in winter, suffer from depression, and can't motivate
themselves.  Apparently it's quite widespread. Only the really bad
clinical cases of it get medical treatment, but lots of people feel a
touch of it. I have a mild dose right now.  Around mid-October every year
(until mid-March) I suddenly discover I need an extra 2-3 hours sleep
each night, and my attention span drops.  I also tend to get
depressed.  I've had this syndrome for about ten years now, and the
past couple of years have been getting worse: to the point where it's
been impacting my work. This year, it began the day before yesterday.
And I am feeling totally drained.

During summer I manage on 6-7 hours of sleep a night. Yesterday I
turned the lights out at 11, was awakened at 7:15 by my alarm, and I
still feel like death. I know that I could sleep until 11am and
_still_ feel like death. This is part and parcel of the syndrome.

Nobody's quite sure where SAD comes from, but there are a couple of
postulated causes. One is a residual hibernation reflex from somewhere
_way_ down the family tree. A more credible answer is a prehistoric 
adaptation to survival in the north (near the Arctic circle) during
winter, when game is scarce. Whatever causes it, it plays merry hell
with me and I want to get rid of it.

I was wondering if anyone else on the list has this problem, and what
strategies you've evolved for dealing with it. One treatment which
apparently works on bad cases is exposure to very intense light;
typically 2000 watts of tungsten bulbs. This weekend I plan to get my
living room fitted with about 250 watts of halogen lights, and will
increase this progressively and see if I feel more wakeful. Has anyone
else got any experience of dealing with SAD, and can you suggest the
best way of fighting it? 

I'm sick of feeling tired all the time ...
