This is true, but the more dramatic examples of the power of the bow are
more modern: bows have about 50% more penetration than the NATO 7.62
cartridge, from the greater mass and sharpness of the arrow. Arrows barely
notice bullet-proof vests. They also produce FAR bigger holes than modern
military rifles, which are designed to wound rather than to kill (killing
is the artillery job.)

>   *       Much easier to aim. (No need for the kind of methods
>	   Henry used to ensure a viable supply of soldiers -- namely
>	   compulsory longbow practice for all freemen in England
>	   every Sunday.)

Indeed, it was said that to train an archer, you had to start with his
grandfather. Modern composite bows are, however, much easier to learn.

Bows are even legal in every jurisdiction I know of.


