Subject: bows and arrows (was: burglary survival)

In <9310131345.AA11569@cfdev1.shearson.com>, Linn Stanton writes:
[...]
|> This is true, but the more dramatic examples of the power of the bow are
|> more modern: bows have about 50% more penetration than the NATO 7.62
|> cartridge, from the greater mass and sharpness of the arrow. Arrows barely
|> notice bullet-proof vests. They also produce FAR bigger holes than modern
|> military rifles, which are designed to wound rather than to kill (killing
|> is the artillery job.)

That is entirely a function of the round you fire (and the context).
Using FMJ in a military rifle in a battlefield situation produces lots
of wounds (the wounded enemy is not returning fire, and that's mainly
what concerns you short-term). Using JHP (hollowpoints) in the same
caliber in a close-up self-defense situation will produce bigger wound
channels, less penetration, therefore better stopping percentage,
which is what you care about.  The higher lethality resulting from the
same factors is the perp's concern, not yours.  "Officer, I was under
attack, I was afraid for my life, and I shot to stop him."

[...]
|> Indeed, it was said that to train an archer, you had to start with his
|> grandfather. Modern composite bows are, however, much easier to learn.

I had forgotten that nice quote. Of course, Extropians may become
their own grandchildren in a few centuries, so it would suffice to
start now. However, no bow is as easy to learn to shoot well as a
quality firearm.

|> Bows are even legal in every jurisdiction I know of.

But concealed carry is such a bitch. I love archery, I practiced a lot
as a teenager, and would still today given time and a nearby range,
but I certainly don't want my life or anyone's to depend on my
bowmanship.
