Just got up this morning and realized something else sobering:

Once upon a time, not only would I not have had a good time, I would
have been _angry_ with people who praised me when I thought I was
doing badly.  ("Can't they _see_ how badly I'm doing?   Of course they
can.  Then why would they say those things?   They must be insulting
my intelligence, or patronizing me (_I_ know I can do better, even if
they don't), or trying to rub it in by pointing out how badly I'm doing
by obvious contradiction.  Well, I won't fall for it -- I'll be
miserable anyway, like I'm supposed to be when I screw up.  And when I
get this game figured out, I'll do better, then I'll actually
_deserve_ some praise.  _Then_ this will be more fun.")  Bleah.  What
a prescription for misery.

This is what a child's version of Objectivism (taught to a child by an
adolescent) can end up looking like.  Don't do it to any kids you know.
I plan not to.

With greater hope as days roll by,
Kennita

