Date: Wed, 13 Oct 1993 11:44:11 -0400 (EDT)
From: LEVY%LENNY@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: ARYAN: NOT

Anton Sherwood writes:

> -- Aryans properly include all the IE-speaking peoples of India and Persia.

There is a lot of confusion about the term "Aryan," which results largely from
the common mistake of confusing language groups (e.g., Indo-European) with
ethnic groups and cultures.  This problem was compounded of course by Hitler's
incorrect and politically motivated usage of the term "Aryan."

What I believe Anton is referring to is the Indo-Aryan subfamily of Indo-
European; An IA language is indeed spoken by the majority of people in 
Iran (Persia) and in the sub-continent.  (You can't "speak Indo-Aryan", any 
more than you can "speak Germanic" or "speak Celtic"; you can only speak a
*language*, like Hindi, English, Gaelic, etc.)  Ancient India, most of whose
inhabitants probably spoke a Dravidian language (the source of modern Tamil, 
Kanada, et al.), were *supposedly* subdued by a group warlike, blond-haired, 
blue-eyed speakers of an Indo-European language.  It is clear that a 
*linguistic* invasion by took place; the racial identity of the invaders is 
much less certain (and, to linguists, of little or no interest).  

There is some scant evidence for common cultural origins in India and Europe, 
including a bizarre ritual of copulation with, and consumption of, a horse, 
recorded in both ancient India and ancient Ireland.  Nevertheless, since 
culture and genetic makeup typically change much faster than language, the 
racial and ethnic origins of the invaders are lost in the mists of time.
Some scholars, like  Harvard linguist Calvert Watkins and archaeologist Marija 
Gimbutas, have tried to mix IE linguistic scholarship with archaeology and 
cultural anthropology, but these sorts of efforts are generally fround upon by
comparative linguists, because the methods of archaeological and cultural
reconstruction are so much less rigorous and developed than those of
linguistic reconstruction.

In short, there is no reason to refer to modern speakers of an Indo-Aryan 
language as "Aryans."

-- Simon!

P.S.: If anyone is interested, I will post (to this list or privately) a
brief introduction to the technique of comparative linguistics, which is
surprisingly simple and precise, and completely independent of considerations
of race and culture.  As I wrote in an earlier posting, I am unaware of any
good introduction to the field in English (most of the scholarship is in
German and French), but I do recommend _The American Heritage Dictionary of
Indo-European Roots_, edited by the aforementioned C. Watkins, which contains
a brief introduction and is full of interesting etymologies that you won't
find in a typical English dictionary.

